For the analysis, we used customers with first payments from February 2026 onwards, where we had sufficiently mature performance.

We assessed the rules across several different bad definitions, including 1@1, 2@2 and 2@3, and only retained rules that performed consistently well across all three. This was to make sure that the final proposals were robust rather than driven by a single performance measure.

We then used PRAISE in two ways.

First, for new rule generation, we took all available Experian data — around 1,000 variables — and used PRAISE to systematically test combinations of variables and thresholds across thousands of iterations.

This allowed us to identify a rule that had not previously been found, relating to customers with a relatively low number of successfully settled good accounts.

The rationale is quite intuitive. Customers with fewer successfully completed credit agreements have less evidence of established and successfully managed credit history. They may therefore be relatively newer to credit or have a thinner credit history, and within this segment we see that they represent a higher-risk population.

Second, we used PRAISE for rule optimisation, revisiting the rules already implemented in the current strategy to identify opportunities to optimise their scope and thresholds.

After identifying the new rule and optimising the existing rules, we then checked the overlap between them and combined the complementary rules with the optimal Delphi score range.

From this process, we finally proposed two rule changes, both focused on customers with a Delphi score between 900 and 1,025, where we want to strengthen our risk controls.

The first is an extension of an existing rule. It focuses on customers with either higher total credit limits on active current accounts or more than two bank credit cards opened in the last three years. So this is primarily capturing higher current credit exposure and more recent credit demand.

The second is the new PRAISE-generated rule. This looks at the same Delphi range but from a different perspective, identifying customers with fewer than four successfully settled good accounts across relevant credit types. This is more focused on the depth and strength of the customer’s established credit history.

So the two rules are complementary: one looks more at current exposure and recent credit demand, while the other looks at evidence of successfully managed credit over time.

The result is a more precise targeting of higher-risk customers, rather than applying a broad Delphi 1,000 cut-off across the population.
